"site-breadcrumbs usage" is a notebook page
	<header level="1">
		Site breadcrumbs
		<header>Site breadcrumbs say where you are in a site:  the group, the pages above the page shown, then the page, each page above a link.</>
	</>
	<section>
		<toolbar class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Put it at the start of an app's top bar.
			Inside a site with a `<ui-site-nav>`, that's all:  with no `pages` of its own, it follows the navigation around it,
			its pages and the page it marks shown, as `<ui-site-tabs>` does.
			A Spell site's page layout writes a bare `<site-breadcrumbs slot="start" />` in its app menu.

			- Elsewhere, hand it the same `pages` you hand `<ui-site-nav>`, and `current`.
			- A group (`band: "group"`) or a page with no `href` is only a name;
			  a page above the one shown is a link, unless its address has a parameter.
			- Each crumb is a real link:  a site that shows its pages itself takes the click as it takes any link to one of its pages.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const crumbs = document.querySelector("ui-site-breadcrumbs")
			crumbs.pages = site.pages
			crumbs.current = location.pathname
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>Each link is a stop for Tab;  Enter follows it.</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			The trail is a `nav` landmark named `label`, else "Where you are";  the page shown is `aria-current="page"`.
			Not found, nothing is drawn, so nothing is announced.
		</>
	</>
